// An undeclared type in a BODY must be rejected by kama itself, with a kama-level message — not sail
// through analysis and surface later as a C-level "undeclared identifier" against generated code.
// (`kama check` and the LSP share the analysis path, so a silent pass here means the editor shows a
// clean file that then fails to build.) The sibling case — a type that EXISTS but isn't imported — is
// reported with the namespace to import; see checkTypeResolves.
fn int main()
{
    Nonexistent thing;
    return 42;
}
